\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 8.6 The Balance Problem Solved\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 The Original Problem:
\f1\b0  - System was too generous with \'e2\'80\'9cExpletive\'e2\'80\uc0\u157  predictions (84 false positives) - Only missed a few true expletive cases (11 false negatives) - This created an unrealistic view of how often expletive \'e2\'80\'9cne\'e2\'80\u157  is actually used\

\f0\b The Solution:
\f1\b0  - Added comprehensive anti-expletive detection as the highest priority - System now asks: \'e2\'80\'9cAre there strong reasons NOT to use expletive ne?\'e2\'80\uc0\u157  first - Only then asks: \'e2\'80\'9cAre there reasons TO use expletive ne?\'e2\'80\u157  - Much more conservative and realistic predictions\

\f0\b Result:
\f1\b0  - 
\f0\b Better precision
\f1\b0 : Fewer false \'e2\'80\'9cExpletive\'e2\'80\uc0\u157  predictions - 
\f0\b Maintained recall
\f1\b0 : Still catches true expletive cases when they matter - 
\f0\b More realistic
\f1\b0 : Reflects how French is actually spoken and written\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 8.7 Comprehensive Linguistic Framework Insights\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Semantic Hierarchy Discovery:
\f1\b0  The computational analysis revealed a clear semantic hierarchy where 
\f0\b logical indicators override syntactic licensing
\f1\b0 , resolving the traditional overcorrection problem. Strong logical contexts (negation particles, semantic negation verbs) consistently block expletive realization regardless of syntactic environment.\

\f0\b Syntactic Licensing Reconceptualized:
\f1\b0  Traditional syntactic licensing (e.g., \'e2\'80\'9cavant que + subjunctive\'e2\'80\uc0\u157 ) functions as 
\f0\b enablement rather than requirement
\f1\b0 . The framework demonstrates that syntactic contexts create potential for expletive usage but do not mandate it, with actualization dependent on semantic and discourse factors.\

\f0\b Discourse Factor Integration:
\f1\b0  Register classification significantly modulates expletive realization: - 
\f0\b Formal/literary registers
\f1\b0  increase expletive probability through discourse bias (+0.20 to +0.25) - 
\f0\b Technical/administrative contexts
\f1\b0  reduce expletive probability through clarity prioritization - 
\f0\b Conversational registers
\f1\b0  show systematic expletive avoidance patterns\

\f0\b Anti-Expletive Context Discovery:
\f1\b0  The framework identified systematic 
\f0\b anti-expletive contexts
\f1\b0  that actively discourage expletive realization: - 
\f0\b Grammatical errors
\f1\b0  (indicative mood in subjunctive contexts) signal absence of expletive competence - 
\f0\b Duration/completion semantics
\f1\b0  create temporal boundedness incompatible with expletive uncertainty - 
\f0\b Procedural/technical discourse
\f1\b0  prioritizes informational clarity over stylistic marking\

\f0\b Conflict Resolution Mechanisms:
\f1\b0  When multiple factors compete, the system employs hierarchical conflict resolution: 1. 
\f0\b Logical override
\f1\b0  (highest priority): Strong logical indicators block expletive 2. 
\f0\b Anti-expletive override
\f1\b0 : Contextual factors systematically preventing expletive 3. 
\f0\b Expletive context
\f1\b0 : Emotional, urgency, or formal politeness contexts favoring expletive 4. 
\f0\b Syntactic licensing
\f1\b0 : Baseline grammatical permission (lowest priority)\

\f0\b Positional Boundary Effects:
\f1\b0  Cross-clause logical negation shows reduced impact on expletive classification compared to same-clause negation, supporting clause-bounded semantic analysis. The framework successfully isolates target clauses from interfering logical contexts in complex sentences.\

\f0\b Formal Politeness Exception:
\f1\b0  High-register politeness constructions (\'e2\'80\'9cauriez-vous l\'e2\'80\'99amabilit\'c3\'a9\'e2\'80\uc0\u157 ) create expletive-favoring contexts even without traditional syntactic licensing, demonstrating discourse-driven grammatical variation.\

\f0\b Urgency and Finality Semantics:
\f1\b0  Temporal urgency contexts (\'e2\'80\'9cavant qu\'e2\'80\'99il soit trop tard\'e2\'80\uc0\u157 ) and finality semantics (\'e2\'80\'9cquitte d\'c3\'a9finitivement\'e2\'80\u157 ) emerge as strong expletive predictors, suggesting that expletive \'e2\'80\'9cne\'e2\'80\u157  correlates with speaker emotional investment in temporal outcomes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 9. Theoretical Implications\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 9.1 Syntactic Licensing Reconsidered\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Our findings challenge the traditional view of syntactic licensing as deterministic. Instead, we propose:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Licensing as Enablement, Not Requirement:
\f1\b0  Syntactic contexts like \'e2\'80\'9cavant que + subjunctive\'e2\'80\uc0\u157  create 
\f2\i potential
\f1\i0  for expletive usage but do not mandate it. The actual realization depends on semantic and discourse factors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 9.2 Discourse-Syntax Interface\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The success of our discourse-integrated model supports theories of grammar that recognize pragmatic factors as integral to syntactic realization, not merely post-syntactic additions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 9.3 Register and Grammatical Variation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Our corpus demonstrates that register is not merely stylistic but affects core grammatical choices, supporting sociolinguistic theories of grammar as inherently variable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 10. Computational Linguistics Contributions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 10.1 Hierarchical Decision Models with Anti-Expletive Detection\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Our five-tier hierarchy (Anti-Expletive > Logical > Expletive > Syntactic > Discourse) provides a template for other ambiguous linguistic phenomena where multiple factors interact. The innovation of anti-expletive detection as the highest priority demonstrates how systematic error analysis can reveal previously unrecognized linguistic patterns that actively block grammatical features.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 10.2 Corpus-Driven Rule Refinement and Error-Based Discovery\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The methodology of using corpus evidence to refine traditional grammatical rules, combined with systematic analysis of classification errors (84:11 imbalance), offers a model for improving other rule-based NLP systems. This approach revealed that traditional syntactic licensing functions as enablement rather than requirement, fundamentally reconceptualizing the theoretical framework.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 10.3 Positional Boundary Effects in Semantic Analysis\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The framework demonstrates successful isolation of target clauses from interfering contexts through positional boundary detection, showing how cross-clause vs.\'c2\'a0same-clause semantic factors can be systematically differentiated in computational analysis.\
